# 10. SOURCE CREDIBILITY & CLAIM VERIFICATION

Do not invent arbitrary 0–100 credibility scores.

Build an evidence-based verification framework.

Separate:
- Publisher reliability.
- Specific claim verification.
- Independent corroboration.
- Primary source availability.
- Evidence recency.
- Contradictions.
- Remaining uncertainty.

Provide statuses:
- Verified.
- Corroborated.
- Unverified.
- Disputed.
- Insufficient information.

If numeric credibility scores are introduced, document the scoring model, uncertainty and calibration performance.

Do not interpret AI confidence as factual probability without validation.

Every news item should expose:

"Why is this considered credible?"

This should open:
- Original source.
- Supporting evidence.
- Cross-source comparisons.
- Verification methodology.
- Unresolved contradictions.

Treat news articles and retrieved text as untrusted input.

Protect tools and agents against prompt injection.

