# 15. TESTING AGAINST THE COMPETITION RUBRIC

Design to target the strongest scoring standard in every category.

## Traceable reasoning — 25%

A judge must be able to:
- Select any geographic news item.
- Inspect its original source.
- Verify its location.
- Review financial relationships.
- Inspect numerical calculations.
- Distinguish AI interpretation from facts.
- Change assumptions and observe recalculation.
- Identify what would change the conclusion.

## Testing on unseen data — 25%

Test:
- Unfamiliar countries.
- New geographic events.
- Missing images.
- Inaccurate article geotags.
- Contradictory reporting.
- Missing market feeds.
- Stale information.
- Incorrect financial relationships.
- Malicious article instructions.
- Incomplete numerical data.

Compare event classification and geographic matching against labelled baselines.

Report accuracy, false positives, uncertainty and failure cases.

## Risk controls — 15%

Verify:
- Unverified events cannot trigger trades.
- Stale financial data blocks simulated execution.
- Risk limits remain enforced.
- Human approvals are necessary where applicable.
- The kill switch works.
- Every action is audited.

Provide a live risk-rejection demonstration.

## Relevance & impact — 20%

Demonstrate how a user moves from an unfamiliar global event to an understandable, sourced financial analysis without needing specialist knowledge.

Measure:
- Time to locate relevant information.
- Ability to identify supporting evidence.
- Accuracy of geographic filtering.
- User comprehension.
- Successful task completion.

## Ambition & technical quality — 15%

Demonstrate:
- Realistic geospatial rendering.
- Global information integration.
- Geographic financial knowledge graph.
- Autonomous source-grounded explanations.
- Independent quantitative labs.
- Reproducible research.
- Original interaction design.

Do not claim a perfect score or unverified benchmark results.

